# DOTS/ECS Architecture Rules for Spellwright

This file defines mandatory rules for implementation using Unity DOTS (Data-Oriented Technology Stack) and ECS (Entity Component System). These rules ensure maximum performance and architectural consistency.

## Rule 1: ISystem vs SystemBase - Mandatory Choice

### ALWAYS Use ISystem For:

- Spell simulation logic executed frame-by-frame
- Movement systems (MovementSystem)
- Collision systems (CollisionSystem)
- Lifetime systems (LifetimeSystem)
- Behavior systems (HomingSystem, ChainingSystem, etc.)
- Payload resolution systems (PayloadResolutionSystem)
- Any performance-critical logic

### ONLY Use SystemBase For:

- JSON baking to entities (SpellBakingSystem)
- One-time initial configuration
- Network API integration
- Communication with traditional UI layer
- Tasks that occur once per spell

### Trade-offs Comparison

| Feature                | ISystem        | SystemBase       | Guideline                 |
| ---------------------- | -------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------- |
| **Burst Compatible**   | ✅ Yes         | ❌ No            | Mandatory for simulation  |
| **Garbage Collection** | ✅ Zero GC     | ⚠️ Potential GC  | Avoid GC in hot paths     |
| **Storage**            | Unmanaged only | Can have managed | Keep simulation pure      |
| **Performance**        | ⚡ Maximum     | 🐌 Moderate      | Performance is critical   |
| **Syntax**             | More verbose   | Simpler          | Performance > Convenience |

## Rule 2: Components - Unmanaged Only

### Correct Component Structure

```csharp
// ✅ CORRECT - Unmanaged component
public struct Velocity : IComponentData
{
    public float3 Value;
}

public struct Lifetime : IComponentData
{
    public float RemainingTime;
    public float InitialTime;
}

public struct HomingModifier : IComponentData
{
    public float TurnSpeed;
    public Entity Target;
}
```

### What to NEVER Do

```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - Managed class
public class Velocity : IComponentData
{
    public Vector3 Value; // Managed type
}

// ❌ WRONG - String in struct
public struct SpellData : IComponentData
{
    public string SpellName; // Strings are managed!
}

// ❌ WRONG - Object reference
public struct SpellConfig : IComponentData
{
    public ScriptableObject Config; // Managed reference!
}
```

### Component Rules

- Use **only blittable types**: int, float, bool, float3, Entity, etc.
- **NEVER** use: string, class, List<T>, Dictionary<K,V>, or any managed type
- Use **FixedString** if you need text (e.g., FixedString64Bytes)
- Use **DynamicBuffer<T>** for variable-size collections
- Keep components **small and focused** (Single Responsibility)

## Rule 3: Systems - Structure and Patterns

### ISystem Pattern

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MovementSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // System initialization
        state.RequireForUpdate<Velocity>();
    }

    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        // Query and processing
        foreach (var (transform, velocity) in
                 SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<LocalTransform>, RefRO<Velocity>>())
        {
            transform.ValueRW.Position += velocity.ValueRO.Value * deltaTime;
        }
    }

    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnDestroy(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Cleanup if needed
    }
}
```

### SystemBase Pattern (Baking Only)

```csharp
public partial class SpellBakingSystem : SystemBase
{
    protected override void OnUpdate()
    {
        // Process JSON and create entities
        var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer(Allocator.TempJob);

        Entities
            .WithAll<SpellJsonData>()
            .ForEach((Entity entity, in SpellJsonData jsonData) =>
            {
                // Create spell entity
                Entity spellEntity = ecb.CreateEntity();
                ecb.AddComponent(spellEntity, new ProjectileTag());
                ecb.AddComponent(spellEntity, new Velocity { Value = jsonData.Speed });

                // Destroy temporary JSON entity
                ecb.DestroyEntity(entity);
            })
            .WithoutBurst()
            .Run();

        ecb.Playback(EntityManager);
        ecb.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Rule 4: EntityCommandBuffer - Structural Changes

### ALWAYS Use ECB For:

- Creating entities
- Destroying entities
- Adding components
- Removing components
- Instantiating prefabs

### Correct Pattern

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct LifetimeSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer(Allocator.TempJob);
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        foreach (var (lifetime, entity) in
                 SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<Lifetime>>()
                         .WithEntityAccess())
        {
            lifetime.ValueRW.RemainingTime -= deltaTime;

            if (lifetime.ValueRO.RemainingTime <= 0)
            {
                // Queued structural change
                ecb.DestroyEntity(entity);
            }
        }

        ecb.Playback(state.EntityManager);
        ecb.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Rule 5: Jobs and Parallelization

### When to Use Jobs

- Heavy mathematical calculations
- Processing large quantities of entities
- Independent operations on entities

### IJobEntity Pattern

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ApplyVelocityJob : IJobEntity
{
    public float DeltaTime;

    [BurstCompile]
    public void Execute(ref LocalTransform transform, in Velocity velocity)
    {
        transform.Position += velocity.Value * DeltaTime;
    }
}

// System usage
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MovementSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var job = new ApplyVelocityJob
        {
            DeltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime
        };

        job.ScheduleParallel();
    }
}
```

## Rule 6: DynamicBuffer for Collections

### When to Use DynamicBuffer

- Variable-size lists (e.g., multiple payloads)
- Dynamic arrays per entity
- Data that changes size at runtime

### Example: Multiple Payloads

```csharp
// Define buffer element
public struct PayloadElement : IBufferElementData
{
    public PayloadType Type;      // Enum: Damage, Heal, ApplyStatus
    public ElementalType Element; // Enum: Fire, Frost, Shock
    public StatusEffectType Effect; // Enum: Burn, Slow, Stun
    public float Amount;
    public float Duration;
}

// Add buffer to entity (in baking)
var buffer = ecb.AddBuffer<PayloadElement>(spellEntity);
buffer.Add(new PayloadElement
{
    Type = PayloadType.Damage,
    Element = ElementalType.Fire,
    Amount = 10f
});
buffer.Add(new PayloadElement
{
    Type = PayloadType.ApplyStatus,
    Effect = StatusEffectType.Burn,
    Duration = 5f
});

// Process buffer (in system)
foreach (var (buffer, entity) in
         SystemAPI.Query<DynamicBuffer<PayloadElement>>()
                 .WithEntityAccess())
{
    foreach (var payload in buffer)
    {
        // Process each payload
        ApplyPayload(entity, payload);
    }
}
```

## Rule 7: Tags for Efficient Queries

### Use Tags for Categorization

```csharp
// Tags are empty components
public struct ProjectileTag : IComponentData { }
public struct AreaOfEffectTag : IComponentData { }
public struct HomingTag : IComponentData { }

// Efficient query
foreach (var (velocity, entity) in
         SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<Velocity>>()
                 .WithAll<ProjectileTag, HomingTag>()
                 .WithEntityAccess())
{
    // Process only homing projectiles
}
```

## Rule 8: System Execution Order

### Define Explicit Order

```csharp
// System must run BEFORE another
[UpdateBefore(typeof(MovementSystem))]
public partial struct HomingSystem : ISystem { }

// System must run AFTER another
[UpdateAfter(typeof(CollisionSystem))]
public partial struct PayloadResolutionSystem : ISystem { }

// Group of related systems
[UpdateInGroup(typeof(SimulationSystemGroup))]
public partial struct SpellSimulationGroup : ComponentSystemGroup { }
```

## Rule 9: Prefabs in ECS

### Create Entity Prefab

```csharp
// In baking system
Entity prefabEntity = ecb.CreateEntity();
ecb.AddComponent<Prefab>(prefabEntity);
ecb.AddComponent(prefabEntity, new ProjectileTag());
ecb.AddComponent(prefabEntity, new Velocity { Value = float3.zero });
ecb.AddComponent(prefabEntity, new Lifetime { InitialTime = 5f });

// Instantiate prefab (extremely fast)
Entity instance = ecb.Instantiate(prefabEntity);
```

## Important Notes

- **ISystem is mandatory** for all spell simulation logic
- **SystemBase** should be used ONLY for baking and initial configuration
- **Managed components** are prohibited for runtime entities
- **EntityCommandBuffer** is mandatory for structural changes
- **Burst Compiler** must be applied to all ISystem and Jobs
- Use **DynamicBuffer** for variable-size collections
- Always define **execution order** when there are dependencies
- **Tags** are more efficient than components with data for queries
- **Prefabs** in ECS are instantiated extremely quickly
- Consult Unity's debug windows (Entities Hierarchy, Systems Window) frequently
